<div class="page-header">
 <div class="w">
  <h1>Undergraduate Syllabus</h1>
  <p>B.Sc. Physics curriculum structured under the Four Year Undergraduate Programme (FYUGP) of Gauhati University, effective from academic session 2023–24.</p>
  <div class="badge-row">
   <span class="badge">FYUGP Gauhati University</span>
   <span class="badge amber">Semester 1 to Semester 6</span>
   <span class="badge">Theory &amp; Practical Credits</span>
  </div>
 </div>
</div>

<main id="top">
<section id="syllabus">
 <div class="w">
  <h2>Course Structure &amp; Papers</h2>
  <p class="lead">Expand each semester below to view core papers, theory/practical credit weightage, and detailed syllabus topics across all six semesters.</p>
  
  <div class="sems">
   <details class="sem" open>
    <summary>Semester 1</summary>
    <div class="courses">
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Mathematical Physics and Mechanics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY101 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Vector calculus</li>
       <li>Curvilinear coordinates</li>
       <li>Dirac delta function</li>
       <li>Reference frames</li>
       <li>Gravitation and central force motion</li>
       <li>Conservation laws</li>
       <li>Dynamics of rigid bodies</li>
       <li>Work and energy</li>
       <li>Oscillations</li>
       <li>Properties of matter</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
    </div>
   </details>

   <details class="sem">
    <summary>Semester 2</summary>
    <div class="courses">
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Mathematical Physics and Electricity and Magnetism</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY151 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Differential equations</li>
       <li>Matrices</li>
       <li>Electric field and electric potential</li>
       <li>Dielectric properties of matter</li>
       <li>Magnetic field</li>
       <li>Magnetic properties of matter</li>
       <li>Electrical circuits</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
    </div>
   </details>

   <details class="sem">
    <summary>Semester 3</summary>
    <div class="courses">
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Waves and Optics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY201 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Superposition of harmonic oscillations</li>
       <li>Wave motion</li>
       <li>Velocity of waves</li>
       <li>Superposition of two harmonic waves</li>
       <li>Wave optics</li>
       <li>Interference</li>
       <li>Diffraction</li>
       <li>Polarization</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
    </div>
   </details>

   <details class="sem">
    <summary>Semester 4</summary>
    <div class="courses">
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Classical Mechanics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY251 &middot; Theory 4 credits</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Lagrangian approach</li>
       <li>Hamiltonian approach</li>
       <li>Small oscillations</li>
       <li>Special theory of relativity</li>
       <li>Fluid dynamics</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Quantum Mechanics I</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY252 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Origin of quantum theory</li>
       <li>Dynamical variables as operators and uncertainty principle</li>
       <li>Matter wave and wave-particle duality</li>
       <li>Schrödinger equation and its applications</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Analog Electronics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY253 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Semiconductor diodes</li>
       <li>Two-terminal devices and their applications</li>
       <li>Bipolar junction transistors</li>
       <li>Amplifiers</li>
       <li>Coupled amplifier</li>
       <li>Feedback in amplifiers</li>
       <li>Sinusoidal oscillators</li>
       <li>Operational amplifiers</li>
       <li>Applications of op-amps</li>
       <li>Introduction to CRO</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Mathematical Physics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY254 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Partial differential equations</li>
       <li>Fourier series</li>
       <li>Complex analysis</li>
       <li>Tensor algebra</li>
       <li>Introduction to probability</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
    </div>
   </details>

   <details class="sem">
    <summary>Semester 5</summary>
    <div class="courses">
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Atomic and Molecular Physics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY301 &middot; Theory 4 credits</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Atom models</li>
       <li>X-rays</li>
       <li>Multi-electron atoms</li>
       <li>Molecular spectra</li>
       <li>Raman effect</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Condensed Matter Physics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY302 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Crystal structure and bonding in solids</li>
       <li>Elementary lattice dynamics</li>
       <li>Dielectric and ferroelectric properties</li>
       <li>Transport properties of materials</li>
       <li>Nanophysics and soft matter</li>
       <li>Magnetic properties of matter</li>
       <li>Superconductivity</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Heat and Thermodynamics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY303 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Distribution of velocities and molecular collisions</li>
       <li>Real gases</li>
       <li>Principles of thermodynamics</li>
       <li>Entropy</li>
       <li>Thermodynamic potentials and relations</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Electromagnetic Theory</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY304 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Maxwell's equations</li>
       <li>EM wave propagation in unbounded media</li>
       <li>EM waves in bounded media</li>
       <li>Polarization of EM waves</li>
       <li>Rotary polarization</li>
       <li>Optical fibres</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
    </div>
   </details>

   <details class="sem">
    <summary>Semester 6</summary>
    <div class="courses">
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Nuclear and Particle Physics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY351 &middot; Theory 4 credits</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Basic properties of nuclei</li>
       <li>Radioactivity and radioactive laws</li>
       <li>Nuclear instrumentation</li>
       <li>Fission and fusion</li>
       <li>Elementary particles</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Digital Electronics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY352 &middot; Theory 3 credits + Practical 1 credit</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Integrated circuits</li>
       <li>Digital circuits</li>
       <li>Boolean algebra</li>
       <li>Arithmetic circuits</li>
       <li>Timers: IC 555</li>
       <li>Sequential circuits</li>
       <li>Shift registers</li>
       <li>Computer organization</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Astronomy and Astrophysics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY353 &middot; Theory 4 credits</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Fundamentals of astronomy</li>
       <li>Astronomical techniques</li>
       <li>Stellar astrophysics</li>
       <li>The solar system</li>
       <li>Galaxies and cosmology</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
     <article class="course">
      <h3>Statistical Mechanics</h3>
      <p class="meta">PHY354 &middot; Theory 4 credits</p>
      <ul>
       <li>Classical statistics</li>
       <li>Classical and quantum theory of radiation</li>
       <li>Bose-Einstein statistics</li>
       <li>Fermi-Dirac statistics</li>
      </ul>
     </article>
    </div>
   </details>
  </div>

  <div class="syllabus-info">
   <div class="info-card">
    <h4>Credit System (FYUGP)</h4>
    <p>Each semester features a balanced combination of theory core papers and laboratory practical credits as prescribed by Gauhati University.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="info-card">
    <h4>Continuous Internal Evaluation</h4>
    <p>Theory courses include sessional exams, home assignments, and seminars; practical courses include regular lab journals and viva voce.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="info-card">
    <h4>Curriculum Document</h4>
    <p>Students can download the official university syllabus PDF for course outcomes and recommended textbooks.</p>
   </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:32px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">
   <a class="btn a" href="Physics_Syllabus.pdf" download>
